  "rationale": "J.J. Johnson single-handedly modernized the jazz trombone, and The Eminent Jay Jay Johnson, Volume 1 is his defining Blue Note statement \u2014 the album that established the trombone as a credible bebop/hard-bop lead voice. The Penguin Guide to Jazz awards it a Crown (4\u2605) calling it 'one of the central documents of post-war jazz.' Compiling two 1953\u20131954 sessions across two NYC-area studios, it features a breathtaking lineup: Clifford Brown in his first studio date, Jimmy Heath on tenor/baritone, John Lewis on piano, the Heath/Clarke rhythm section, plus the 1954 session team of Wynton Kelly, Charles Mingus, and Kenny Clarke. AllMusic rates it 4.5\u2605. J.J. Johnson is completely absent from the current canon; this is the foundational entry point for the instrument and the musician.",
