  "ballot": {
    "album": "J.J. Johnson \u2014 The Eminent Jay Jay Johnson, Volume 1 (1955)",
    "scope_check": {
      "in_window": true,
      "boundary_note": null
    },
    "tier": "consensus_core",
    "priority": "must_have",
    "case_for": "A Penguin Guide Crown \u2014 'one of the central documents of post-war jazz' \u2014 corroborated by AllMusic's 4.5 stars makes this the consensus-defining statement of the trombone's modernization: the post-1950 hard-bop trombone lineage (Curtis Fuller, Slide Hampton, Julian Priester) traces directly to these sessions, and the canon currently has no Johnson and no trombone chair at all. Session gravity is exceptional: the 1953 date is one of Clifford Brown's earliest studio appearances alongside John Lewis, Jimmy Heath, Percy Heath, and Kenny Clarke, while the 1954 date pairs Wynton Kelly with Charles Mingus and Clarke behind the leader. A cold listener gets two instructive things at once \u2014 the trombone as a fully articulate bop lead voice, and Brown still finding his language \u2014 making it both the instrument's anchor and a discovery-grade record.",
    "case_against": "The record is a compilation, not a conceived long-player: BLP 1505 stitches the 1953 sextet 10-inch (BLP 5028, with Brown) and the 1954 quintet 10-inch (BLP 5057) into one package whose personnel barely overlap \u2014 Johnson and Clarke only \u2014 with different studios and rhythm sections, so the Crown judges an assemblage rather than a unified statement. Johnson's natively conceived 12-inch dates (Dial J.J. and Blue Trombone, both 1957) are arguably more organic career peaks, and a purist could prefer one of those as the entry point.",
    "disagreement": null,
    "recommendation": "The council proposes John include it as consensus_core / must_have \u2014 the trombone tradition's anchor and a Crown-certified foundational Blue Note date.",
    "confidence": "high",
